 Since $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ are orthogonal, we can decompose the vector $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}$ into orthogonal components parallel and perpendicular to $\mathbf{a}$. The projection of $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}$ onto $\mathbf{a}$ is given as $\begin{pmatrix} -\frac{3}{5} \\ -\frac{6}{5} \end{pmatrix}$. The projection of $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}$ onto $\mathbf{b}$ will be perpendicular to $\mathbf{a}$, and since $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ are orthogonal, we can find the distance of the vector $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}$ from the line spanned by $\mathbf{a}$, which is the component parallel to $\mathbf{b}$. The length of this component is the magnitude of the vector $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}$ projected onto $\mathbf{a}$. The magnitude of the projection of $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}$ onto $\mathbf{a}$ is $\sqrt{\left(-\frac{3}{5}\right)^2 + \left(-\frac{6}{5}\right)^2} = \sqrt{\frac{9}{25} + \frac{36}{25}} = \sqrt{\frac{45}{25}} = \frac{3\sqrt{5}}{5}$. Thus, the projection of $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}$ onto $\mathbf{b}$ is $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} -\frac{3}{5} \\ -\frac{6}{5} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 + \frac{3}{5} \\ -3 + \frac{6}{5} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{18}{5} \\ -\frac{9}{5} \end{pmatrix}$. Therefore, the projection of $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}$ onto $\mathbf{b}$ is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} \frac{18}{5} \\ -\frac{9}{5} \end{pmatrix}}$. </think> <answer>
\begin{align*}
\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{b}} \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} &= \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} - \operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{a}} \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} -\frac{3}{5} \\ -\frac{6}{5} \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} \frac{18}{5} \\ -\frac{9}{5} \end{pmatrix}}
